From: bonsai-27b-fusion <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(kvmem): turbo3/turbo4 KV cache types, full support

KVMem can now run its KV tier on the TurboQuant cache types.

Only one of the three gates was actually a kernel problem:

  * tools/kvmem/kvmem-spec.cpp: accept turbo3/turbo3_0/turbo4/turbo4_0 in
    kvmem_parse_cache_type(), and whitelist both in kvmem_cache_types_ok().
    --kv-dtype / -ctk / -ctv advertise them, and so does the error string.
  * The host-side codec needed nothing: kvmem_cache_unpack_rows() already
    falls through to ggml_get_type_traits(ty)->to_float, and ggml.c routes
    GGML_TYPE_TURBO3_0/4_0 to quantize_turbo3_0 / dequantize_row_turbo3_0.
  * src/adapter/llama-kvmem-stagein.cu needed a device codec.

The structural reason this is cheap: the tiered store is type-opaque. GPU
cache rows move as per-row byte copies (copy_k_gpu -> kvmem_stagein_enqueue_v
/ _h2d_bytes) inside a row already sized by ggml_row_size(type, n_embd), and
nothing in kvmem/ or the adapter ever interprets those bytes. turbo3/turbo4
rows therefore round-trip with no codec at all, and read_gpu_block /
dump_kv_compare decode through the ggml trait.

The new codec serves the other stage-in shape (kvmem_stagein_enqueue_k),
which rebuilds a row from values: dequant -> RoPE -> KVMem Hadamard ->
requantise. For turbo that last step must land byte-for-byte on what
k_set_rows_turbo3 / k_set_rows_turbo4 would have written, or a restored row
would not match the rows prefill wrote natively into the same cache.

  * dequant_turbo3 / dequant_turbo4: centroid x block norm.
  * quant_turbo3_grouped<128|64>: group L2 norm -> signed FWHT -> nearest
    centroid -> pack 2+1 bits -> reconstruction-corrected norm.
  * quant_turbo4_grouped: same, one 128-element block, nibble packing,
    rnorm written as 0.
  * kvmem_stagein_quant_ok(): widened to both turbo types.

Two deliberate deviations from set-rows.cu:

  * No __shfl_* and no __ballot_*. HIP's wavefront is 64 on GCN and 32 on
    RDNA and this tree targets gfx1030, so every reduction is a shared-memory
    tree and the sub-block packing is addressed in shared memory. The result
    is wavefront-size independent.
  * The WHT group size comes from head_dim, not the row width:
    (n_embd_head % 128 == 0) ? 128 : 64, mirroring the op_params that
    llama-kv-cache.cpp writes. The two rules differ only for head_dim 64 with
    an even KV head count, where the row-width rule would merge two heads
    into one group.

tools/kvmem/gen-turbo-tables.py generates llama-kvmem-turbo-{host,device}.h
from ggml/src/ggml-cuda/turbo-quant.cuh, so the codebooks and the WHT sign
tables cannot drift from ggml's. The emitter writes 9 significant digits: the
first cut used .1f, which looked right for the +/-1.0 sign tables but
collapsed the codebooks (smallest 3-bit centroid 0.021460 -> 0.0).

Verified:
  --kv-dtype turbo3 -ctk turbo3 -ctv turbo4   accepted; fails only on missing model
  --kv-dtype turbo3_0                         accepted (alias)
  --kv-dtype turbo9                           unsupported cache type (...|turbo3|turbo4)
  gen-turbo-tables.py --check                 turbo tables up to date
  ninja llama-server llama-kvmem-server       NINJA_RC=0

Not proven: the device codec compiled but never ran. This sandbox has no ROCm
device, and kvmem_stagein_enqueue_k has no caller - the live stage-in is
write_block_to_gpu(), which copies raw rows. So what carries turbo3/4 at
runtime is the type-opaque byte path; the kernels are compile-verified only.

Refs docs/kvmem/FUSION-NOTES.md, section "TurboQuant KV types".
